<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Persistence for scripts/spin-catcher.sh (AC-171).

  WHY THIS EXISTS: the catcher was armed as a plain background process inside an
  amux session. When that session ends the trap dies silently and nothing says so,
  which is the worst failure mode for a watcher — it reports nothing whether it is
  working or gone. KeepAlive makes "is it running" true by construction instead of
  by someone remembering.

  WHAT IT IS ACTUALLY WATCHING FOR, after 625 captured events corrected the premise:
  NOT a CPU spin. Per-thread CPU showed no hot thread (38.5/14.3/2.6/~0, every
  STAT=S) while /health reported 80-89%, so the process is bursty, not spinning, and
  CPU alone no longer trips. The triggers worth an unattended watcher are the two
  that never once fired across those 625 events and would therefore be real:
    - /health does not answer at all  (the actual reported wedge: board + email hang)
    - store != ok / degraded          (a genuine SQLite stall)

  SAFETY: the catcher only sends SIGUSR1, which makes the server PRINT its stacks.
  It never stops, restarts, or kills anything. This machine runs 24/7 and that rule
  is not bent here.

  NOT LOADED BY THE AGENT THAT WROTE IT. Installing a permanent background job on
  someone's machine is the owner's call, not a side effect of a debugging task:

      launchctl load -w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.amux.spin-catcher.plist

  To install:
      cp scripts/launchd/com.amux.spin-catcher.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
      launchctl load -w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.amux.spin-catcher.plist
  To verify it is actually running (do not trust the load command's exit code):
      launchctl list | grep spin-catcher
      tail -5 ~/.amux/spin-dumps/catcher.out
  To remove:
      launchctl unload -w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.amux.spin-catcher.plist
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.amux.spin-catcher</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/Users/ethan/Dev/amux/scripts/spin-catcher.sh</string>
        <string>3</string>   <!-- poll seconds -->
        <string>70</string>  <!-- cpu trigger, only with sustain below -->
        <string>5</string>   <!-- consecutive polls before believing the CPU gauge -->
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The whole point of the card: survive the session that started it. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- A watcher that respawns instantly on a crash-loop is itself a load source.
         The cloud watchdog sidecar did exactly that: exit 1 under an uncapped
         restart policy, 4318 restarts, dockerd saturated, host dark for an hour
         (AC-162/AC-163). Ten seconds is cheap insurance against repeating it here. -->
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>10</integer>

    <!-- Never /dev/null. A silent watcher cannot be distinguished from a dead one,
         and that ambiguity is the exact defect this card was filed about. -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/ethan/.amux/spin-dumps/catcher.out</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/ethan/.amux/spin-dumps/catcher.err</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>AMUX_URL</key>
        <string>https://localhost:8822</string>
    </dict>

    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
</dict>
</plist>
